-- title:
--   Lemma 14 — time reparameterization
-- statement:
--   Let $T>0$, let $H:[0,T]\to\mathrm{Herm}(\mathcal H_S)$ be $\beta$-Lipschitz in the spectral norm, and let $h:[0,T]\to[0,\infty)$ be $\beta_h$-Lipschitz with $\|H(t)\|\le h(t)$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Assume $\Lambda_h=\int_0^T h(t)\,dt>0$, and define $\mu$, $g$, $F$, $\tau$ and $\widetilde H$ by (88)–(90). Then:
--
--   1. $F$ is a strictly increasing $C^1$ bijection from $[0,T]$ onto $[0,2\Lambda_h]$, and for every $s\in[0,2\Lambda_h]$
--   $$\tau'(s)=\frac{1}{g(\tau(s))}.$$
--   2. $\|\widetilde H(s)\|\le1$ for $0\le s\le2\Lambda_h$.
--   3. There is a constant $\widetilde\beta$ with
--   $$\|\widetilde H(s)-\widetilde H(r)\|\le\widetilde\beta\,|s-r|\quad(s,r\in[0,2\Lambda_h]),\qquad \widetilde\beta\le\frac{(\beta+\beta_h)T^2}{\Lambda_h^2}.$$
--   4. The time-ordered evolution is preserved: $U_{\widetilde H}(2\Lambda_h)=U_H(T)$.
--
--   The lemma converts the original evolution into one generated by a Hamiltonian of norm at most one over the time $2\Lambda_h$, with a controlled Lipschitz constant; this is exactly the input format of the main simulation theorem, which is how Theorem 15 is obtained.
--
--   **Formalization Note** No differentiability of $H$ or $h$ and no positive lower bound on $h$ are assumed. Derivatives on the closed intervals are one-sided at the endpoints. The Lipschitz constants are real numbers; their nonnegativity follows from the hypotheses since $T>0$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: qubit Hilbert space H_S and Herm(H_S) → binary-string basis Bits n, finite complex matrices, the spectral (L2 operator) norm; reason: a change of coordinates representing every source instance; the base mission's convention
--   - encoding: H : [0,T] → Herm(H_S) and h : [0,T] → [0,∞) → functions on ℝ with every hypothesis (Hermitian, Lipschitz, nonnegative, envelope) restricted to [0,T]; reason: values outside [0,T] never enter Λ_h, F, τ, H̃ on their source intervals, or the propagator
--   - encoding: τ := F⁻¹ : [0,2Λ_h] → [0,T] → tau = Function.invFunOn F [0,T], a preimage chosen in [0,T], with an arbitrary default outside F([0,T]); reason: clause 1 makes F a bijection, so on [0,2Λ_h] this is exactly F⁻¹; values outside are never used
--   - encoding: C¹ on [0,T] and τ′(s) on [0,2Λ_h] → ContDiffOn ℝ 1 on Icc 0 T and HasDerivWithinAt on Icc 0 (2Λ_h); reason: the standard closed-interval meaning, one-sided derivatives at the endpoints
--   - encoding: U_H̃(2Λ_h) = U_H(T) → propagator (Htilde H h T) (2Λ_h) (2Λ_h) = propagator H T T, the base mission's selected solution of the initial-value problem; reason: the base definition of U_H; the solution is unique for Lipschitz Hamiltonians
-- source:
--   Boyang Chen, Minbo Gao, Zhengfeng Ji, Tongyang Li, Xinzhao Wang, Shuo Zhou, STOC 2027 submission #383 (October 2026), Lemma 14 (Time reparameterization), pp.36–37; proof pp.37–38. https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/383.pdf

import Definitions.Def_TDHamSim_QueryOptimal_Supplement_Reparam

open scoped Matrix.Norms.L2Operator

namespace TDHamSim.QueryOptimal.Supplement

open TDHamSim.QueryOptimal

/-- Lemma 14, pp.36–37: all four clauses of the time reparameterization. Derivatives on the
closed intervals are one-sided at the endpoints; nothing is assumed outside `[0,T]`. -/
theorem lemma_14 {n : ℕ} (H : ℝ → Mat (Bits n)) (h : ℝ → ℝ) (β βh T : ℝ)
    (hT : 0 < T)
    (hHerm : ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, (H t).IsHermitian)
    (hHLip : ∀ s ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, opNorm (H t - H s) ≤ β * |t - s|)
    (hh0 : ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, 0 ≤ h t)
    (hhLip : ∀ s ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, |h t - h s| ≤ βh * |t - s|)
    (hHh : ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, opNorm (H t) ≤ h t)
    (hΛ : 0 < intNorm h T) :
    -- 1. `F` is a strictly increasing `C¹` bijection `[0,T] → [0,2Λ_h]`, and `τ' = 1/g(τ)`.
    (StrictMonoOn (Fclock h T) (Set.Icc 0 T) ∧
      Set.BijOn (Fclock h T) (Set.Icc 0 T) (Set.Icc 0 (2 * intNorm h T)) ∧
      ContDiffOn ℝ 1 (Fclock h T) (Set.Icc 0 T) ∧
      ∀ s ∈ Set.Icc 0 (2 * intNorm h T),
        HasDerivWithinAt (tau h T) (1 / gReg h T (tau h T s))
          (Set.Icc 0 (2 * intNorm h T)) s) ∧
    -- 2. `‖H̃(s)‖ ≤ 1` on `[0,2Λ_h]`.
    (∀ s ∈ Set.Icc 0 (2 * intNorm h T), opNorm (Htilde H h T s) ≤ 1) ∧
    -- 3. `H̃` is `β̃`-Lipschitz on `[0,2Λ_h]` with `β̃ ≤ (β+β_h)T²/Λ_h²`.
    (∃ βt : ℝ,
      (∀ s ∈ Set.Icc 0 (2 * intNorm h T), ∀ r ∈ Set.Icc 0 (2 * intNorm h T),
        opNorm (Htilde H h T s - Htilde H h T r) ≤ βt * |s - r|) ∧
      βt ≤ (β + βh) * T ^ 2 / intNorm h T ^ 2) ∧
    -- 4. `U_{H̃}(2Λ_h) = U_H(T)`.
    propagator (Htilde H h T) (2 * intNorm h T) (2 * intNorm h T) = propagator H T T := by sorry

end TDHamSim.QueryOptimal.Supplement
